0023-gpui-snapshot-flash-overlay.patch (2026-11-15, rebuilt and compiled). Replaces the 2026-11-15 draft of the
same name (now in nsh-host-patches/superseded-2026-11-13/): that version was a sketch written without the
vendored tree present, ending in `todo!()`. This version was built against the real tree
(third_party/nsh-host-build-0022) and a release binary was produced from it.

Applies with `patch -p1` at the root of a copy of nsh-host-allspeech-src after patches 0010-0022, in order.
Verified: applies cleanly on top of 0022, and `cargo build --release --locked --offline` of `nsh-gpui-host`
succeeds with no new Cargo.lock entries (raw `objc2::msg_send!`/`class!` against `NSWindow`/`NSScreen`/
`NSAnimationContext`, AppKit, already linked — the same convention `native/capture.rs` (patch 0015) uses).

Reference: apps/desktop/src/snapShot/DesktopSnapShot.ts:607-675 (`SnapShotFlash`, `createSnapShotFlashWindow`),
:693-722 (`showCaptureFeedback`, `snapShotFlashBounds`). FLASH_PEAK_OPACITY, FLASH_ANIMATION_DURATION_MS,
FLASH_STATIC_DURATION_MS are that file's own constants, copied verbatim (FLASH_FRAME_INTERVAL_MS is not used: the
fade is driven by one `NSAnimationContext` animation group, not a frame-by-frame timer).

Adds the host call `snapshot.flash { bounds: {x,y,width,height}, animated: boolean }`: shows a borderless,
click-through, always-on-top, white `NSWindow` over `bounds` (screen points, top-left origin, as
desktop/snapShotActiveWindow.ts's `ActiveWindow.bounds` already is — flipped to AppKit's bottom-left origin
against the primary screen's height), resolving once it is shown. When `animated`, its alpha fades to 0 over
FLASH_ANIMATION_DURATION_MS via `NSAnimationContext.runAnimationGroup:completionHandler:`, then closes; otherwise
it holds FLASH_PEAK_OPACITY for FLASH_STATIC_DURATION_MS (a detached thread sleep, then closes on the main thread
via `crate::rt::invoke`) and closes without a fade. Lists `snapshot.flash` in `desktop.capabilities`'s answer, so
a host without this patch is correctly reported as not supporting it (`snapShotFlash.ts`'s capability gate).

Built/verified: `cargo check`/`cargo clippy` (clean) and a release build, both `--locked --offline`; a headless
`nsh test` smoke run got `snapshot.flash` resolving `null` for an animated flash and `desktop.capabilities`
listing the name. NOT verified: what the flash actually looks like on screen (headless tests have no real
display), the fade's visual smoothness, or behavior across multiple displays with different scale factors —
`primary_frame` is only `NSScreen.screens.firstObject`, not necessarily the screen `bounds` is on.

